**How to read the table.** Each row is one input or result. The fingerprint is a short code computed from its exact contents. For the parameter file it is a standard MD5 checksum of the file. For results it is a hash of the numbers themselves, computed in R with `rlang::hash()`. Changing even one value produces a completely different code.

**What the table tells you.** Anyone who reruns `run_all.R` with the restored packages can compute the same fingerprints. If they all match, the rerun reproduced this site's numbers exactly. We checked this by running the pipeline repeatedly, including the parallel 30-seed study, and every fingerprint matched. A mismatch on `params.yaml` means the assumptions changed. A mismatch on results with the same parameters points to a different package version.

## Parameters and seeds {#parameters}

All assumptions live in `params.yaml`, each labelled as an assumption with its range. The seeds are:

- **Main run:** seed 42, used for every single-run plot and table.
- **Robustness study:** seeds 1 to 30, one complete pipeline each.
- **Service graph:** seed 7 for the simulated call volumes.
- **Secondary analyses** in `run_all.R` (gradient check, architecture sweep, bootstrap, drift demo, routing) use fixed offsets from the main seed.

`run_pipeline()` calls `set.seed()` with its own seed at the start, so each run draws the same random numbers whether the 30-seed study runs on one core or many. The page also re-derives the heatmap sample from the main seed, so the "random" incidents shown are the same in every render.

::: {.callout-note icon="false" collapse="true"}
## params.yaml

```yaml
# Every value below is a modelling assumption for the synthetic study, not an
# estimate from real telemetry. Ranges are [min, max] of a uniform draw.

seeds:
  main: 42                 # seed for the main run shown in the plots
  robustness: 30           # seeds 1..30 for the multi-seed robustness study

simulation:
  start_date: "2026-01-05" # a Monday, so day 1 is a weekday
  window_minutes: 5        # one row = one host x 5-minute window
  n_days: 14
  hosts_train: 4           # 4 x 14 days x 288 windows = 16,128 rows
  hosts_val: 4             # held-out normal windows for threshold fitting
  hosts_test: 2            # clean normal windows in the test set
  hosts_attack_pool: 4     # normal windows that attacks are injected into
  ar_phi: 0.5              # AR(1) persistence of every latent factor
  diurnal_amplitude: 2.5   # size of the business-hours bump in activity
  diurnal_peak_hour: 13
  diurnal_width_hours: 3
  weekend_activity: 0.3    # weekend bump as a share of a weekday bump
  n_attacks_per_cell: 150  # per attack type x intensity tier

attacks:
  data_exfiltration:
    label: "Data Exfiltration"
    low:    {bytes_mult: [1.5, 3.0], tls_mult: [1.5, 3.0], entropy_mult: [0.7, 0.9]}
    medium: {bytes_mult: [5, 10],    tls_mult: [5, 10],    entropy_mult: [0.7, 0.9]}
    high:   {bytes_mult: [25, 60],   tls_mult: [8, 20],    entropy_mult: [0.7, 0.9]}
  credential_stuffing:
    label: "Credential Stuffing"
    low:    {ips_add: 4,  ips_pois: 2,  fails_add: 1, fails_pois: 1}
    medium: {ips_add: 15, ips_pois: 5,  fails_add: 3, fails_pois: 2}
    high:   {ips_add: 0,  ips_pois: 80, fails_add: 0, fails_pois: 45}
  privilege_escalation:
    label: "Privilege Escalation"
    low:    {priv_add: 1}
    medium: {priv_add: 2}
    high:   {priv_add: 4}
  ransomware_staging:
    label: "Ransomware Staging"
    low:    {child_add: 3,  child_pois: 2,  fs_add: 6,  fs_pois: 3,  dns_mult: [1.2, 1.6]}
    medium: {child_add: 12, child_pois: 5,  fs_add: 20, fs_pois: 10, dns_mult: [2, 4]}
    high:   {child_add: 0,  child_pois: 35, fs_add: 0,  fs_pois: 60, dns_mult: [4, 10]}
  lateral_movement:
    label: "Lateral Movement"
    low:    {dest_add: 1,  dest_pois: 1, smb_add: 2,  smb_pois: 1,  svc_add: 0, svc_prob: 0.3}
    medium: {dest_add: 3,  dest_pois: 2, smb_add: 6,  smb_pois: 3,  svc_add: 1, svc_prob: 0}
    high:   {dest_add: 10, dest_pois: 5, smb_add: 20, smb_pois: 10, svc_add: 3, svc_prob: 0}

low_and_slow:
  label: "Low-and-Slow Exfiltration"
  n_campaigns: 30
  length: [12, 48]          # consecutive 5-minute windows per campaign (1-4 hours)
  bytes_mult: [1.5, 3.0]
  tls_mult: [1.2, 2.0]
  entropy_mult: [0.7, 0.9]

autoencoder:
  bottleneck: 4
  max_epochs: 60
  patience: 5               # early stopping on validation loss
  batch_size: 256
  lr: 0.005
  sweep_bottleneck: [2, 4, 8]
  deep_hidden: 8            # second architecture: d-8-4-8-d

evt:
  u_prob: 0.95              # POT threshold u = 95th percentile of held-out losses
  q: [0.99, 0.999, 0.9999, 0.99999]
  bootstrap_B: 1000
  decluster_run_length: 3   # runs method: exceedances within 3 windows = one cluster
  min_empirical_exceedances: 10

operations:
  windows_per_day: 1000000  # assumed scoring volume of a large SOC
  prevalence: [1.0e-5, 1.0e-4, 1.0e-3]

graph:
  damping: 0.85
  damping_sensitivity: [0.75, 0.85, 0.95]
  tol: 1.0e-12
  max_iter: 1000
  tier1_removal_impact: 0.5    # policy choice: Tier 1 if removal cuts off >= 50% of services
  seed: 7

drift:
  week_hosts: 100           # 100 hosts x 7 days = production-scale weekly sample
  week_days: 7
  material_shift_sd: 0.3
  harmless_shift_sd: 0.05
  shifted_features: ["net_bytes_out_kb", "host_cpu_utilization"]
  psi_watch: 0.1
  psi_alert: 0.2
  ks_alpha: 0.01
```
